    "uid": "207f6020552874fb6c3019361c7d8c50",
    "title": "PAM configuration modified",
    "desc": "A program edited a PAM configuration file (/etc/pam.d/*, /etc/pam.conf) or referenced a PAM module (pam_*.so) while editing one. Pluggable Authentication Modules decide who may log in and with what; dropping a `pam_succeed_if` rule that waves everyone through, or inserting a malicious module, is a stealthy way to create a backdoor and harvest credentials (MITRE T1556.003) that survives password changes. Editing PAM config is a rare, deliberate act outside of a package upgrade or a config-management run, so by hand on a live host it is a clean signal.",
    "analytic": {
      "type_id": 1,
      "type": "Rule",
      "name": "pam_config_modification",
